# Pull requests

When the user asks to commit, push, or open a PR, treat docs as part of the change, not a follow-up.

## Documentation (every PR)

If behavior, commands, paths, env vars, or architecture changed, update the same commit (or a docs commit on the branch) so these match the code:

- `README.md` — version, current status, commands, layout
- `CHANGELOG.md` and `pyproject.toml` / `__init__.py` (version bump)
- Relevant pages under `docs/` and `plans/` that describe **current** behavior
- `docs/README.md` catalog and `mkdocs.yml` `nav` if you add or rename a doc ([docs/PROCESS.md](docs/PROCESS.md))

Do not leave current docs describing the old system. Historical pages get a Status line; do not mix them with shipped behavior.

A Cursor hook may block `gh pr create` until README and relevant `docs/` / `plans/` match. Fix the docs and commit; do not work around the hook.

## Screenshots (visual changes)

If the PR changes something a human sees — operator UI, landing page, MkDocs chrome, Slack Block Kit layout — add at least one screenshot to the PR body or under `docs/` and link it.

- Capture the changed screen after exercising the flow (not only a first paint)
- Desktop is enough unless the change is layout/responsive
- Skip screenshots for pure backend, indexer, or path-only changes

## PR body

Fill [`.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`](.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md): Summary (what / why), Test plan (checkboxes), and Screenshots when the change is visual. Mention linked issues. Follow [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md). Do not include `landing/` or other unrelated untracked work.
